michelle barnier spoke to ambassadors  this morning i'm told he was  upbeat in that meeting that he said that  a deal was still possible but not  guaranteed he went to the european  parliament  and repeated that message before  resuming his negotiations with  lord frost uh uk's chief negotiator  there was  there perhaps still is a sort of frisson  uh of excitement in the air but let me  tell you in the past  few moments i've received some details  from a source within the uk  government about the british view of  negotiations  and it is a little more downbeat let me  tell you it is this  from a source talks remain difficult and  we have  not made significant progress in recent  days  despite efforts by the uk side to bring  energy and ideas to the process that is  of course the view from the uk  it carries on like any sovereign country  we must have the right to take our own  decisions  choose regulations that suit the uk we  cannot sign up to dynamic alignment  through the back door  it goes on the uk cannot be locked into  eu's regulatory  orbit this is clearly in relation to  negotiations around the level  playing field and that question of  whether  the uk would face some kind of  punishment as it were  if it chose to diverge from eu  regulations that is something where i  was told  a great deal of focus had been placed in  negotiations but not only that in  on fishing uh this source saying we've  always been clear the uk  will have control over access to our  waters and a better deal for uk fishing  communities  there is says this source simply no  truth to the idea  that we have backtracked that was a  suggestion that was made this morning  that the uk  had in some way gone soft uh on its  tough stance over fishing  and i think that uh rather downbeat  assessment from a uk government source  does reduce that sense of optimism  because  while the uk and the eu know each  other's positions very well  it is hard to see at this stage how you  manufacture a deal  if there is no give on fishing  now there is a great deal riding on this  d  on this set of negotiations you heard  from michelle barnier  a little earlier you've also heard today  from ursula von der line the president  of the european commission giving her  assessment  not just of the generality of brexit  talks but also  about the specifics of the level playing  field here's what she had to say  we're on the very last mile to go  but it's an essential one and we want a  level playing field  we want a level playing field not only  at the start but  also over time and this is the  architecture we are building  and we are fine about the architecture  itself  but the details in it do they really fit  and these are crucial points  because again it's a matter of fairness  it's a matter of fair competition  and we want to ensure that  we have a curious blend then at the  moment uh we have  the european commission and the  negotiators who do feel as if today they  have been  a little more upbeat uh one eu diplomat  saying  that that person could see what he  described as a narrow  path towards an agreement we now have we  know the uk striking a much more  pessimistic tone  and also the third spoke on this wheel  is the european parliament which is  beginning to flex its muscles  about its requirement to be involved in  the  the ratification process of any deal  uh it may be that a deal that is struck  at the last minute won't have time to go  through  the parliament there was and brussels is  a great city for rumors a  story that has gone around in the past  hour suggesting uh that some of the  biggest parties in the parliament  had passed a motion agreed not to do any  ratification of a brexit deal i'm told  by sources that that is  not correct but it does i think reflect  uh a complicated picture the parliament  feels it was marginalized in previous  discussions around the withdrawal  agreement  does not want to be seen as simply  rubber stamping  any future trade deal 